  "physics_configuration": {
    "method": "2D TorchFDTD run (period along x, propagation along +y, ridges along the invariant z) with Bloch boundaries on x, native CPML on y, a soft Bloch plane sheet below the grating and three line monitors: incident (empty run only), reflection between source and grating, transmission beyond the grating. The lines are lifted to a two-sample z-invariant plane and decomposed with the native torchfdtd.radiation.diffraction_orders (E and H separation into forward and backward Rayleigh orders). Efficiency = order power / forward zero-order power of the incident monitor in the matched empty-cell run; the reflected field is the sample minus the empty-cell field. Phases: transmitted amplitude propagated from the transmission line to the grating exit face, reflected amplitude propagated from the reflection line to the grating entrance face, both divided by the incident amplitude propagated from the incident line to the entrance face, using the continuum k_y of each order; TE compares Ez, TM compares Ex. Interface method subpixel (axis-aligned faces have exact fractions), Yee material sampling; the staircase interface is recorded as a control.",
    "period_um": 1.2,
    "fill": 0.5,
    "height_um": 0.5,
    "index": 2.0,
    "superstrate_and_substrate": "vacuum on both sides (freestanding grating)",
    "wavelengths_um": [
      0.92,
      1.02,
      1.06
    ],
    "angles_deg": [
      0.0,
      20.0
    ],
    "wavelength_selection": "chosen from a TORCWA zero-order scan (0.55 to 1.2 um, both angles) so that no propagating order lies within 10 percent of grazing and no sharp guided-mode resonance (width below 0.02 um) falls on the three points; the scan is not part of the record",
    "incidence": "the Bloch wavevector is fixed per run at k_x = k_0 sin(20 deg) of the run's own wavelength, so each oblique run is judged at its own wavelength only (fixed-k Bloch injection is not fixed-angle); the normal-incidence runs are judged at all three wavelengths",
    "propagating_orders": {
